---
title: RabbitMQ consumer
description: Turning a queue message into a dispatch — the scope, the typed lane, and why the consumer should not use the unified mediator.
---

A queue consumer reads bytes and has to turn them into a dispatch. The mechanics are
straightforward; the parts worth getting right are the DI scope, the acknowledgement boundary,
and keeping enough static type information that the compiler can still help you.

## The shape

```csharp
public sealed class OrderConsumer(
    IServiceScopeFactory scopeFactory,
    ILogger<OrderConsumer> logger) : BackgroundService
{
    protected override async Task ExecuteAsync(CancellationToken stoppingToken)
    {
        await foreach (var delivery in _channel.ReadAllAsync(stoppingToken))
        {
            using var scope = scopeFactory.CreateScope();
            var mediator = scope.ServiceProvider.GetRequiredService<ICommandMediator>();

            try
            {
                var command = JsonSerializer.Deserialize<PlaceOrder>(delivery.Body.Span)!;
                await mediator.SendAsync(command, stoppingToken);
                await _channel.BasicAckAsync(delivery.DeliveryTag, false, stoppingToken);
            }
            catch (Exception ex)
            {
                logger.LogError(ex, "delivery {Tag} failed", delivery.DeliveryTag);
                await _channel.BasicNackAsync(delivery.DeliveryTag, false, requeue: false, stoppingToken);
            }
        }
    }
}
```

## One scope per message

This is the part to get right. A `BackgroundService` is a singleton, so it cannot inject a
mediator directly — the handler's scoped dependencies would come from the root scope and live
forever.

```csharp
using var scope = scopeFactory.CreateScope();
var mediator = scope.ServiceProvider.GetRequiredService<ICommandMediator>();
```

One scope per delivery gives each message its own `DbContext`, its own unit of work, and
disposal when it finishes — the same lifetime an HTTP request would get.

## Keep the static type as specific as you can

A consumer that knows what it deserialized should dispatch through the specific mediator:

```csharp
var command = JsonSerializer.Deserialize<PlaceOrder>(delivery.Body.Span)!;
await mediator.SendAsync(command, stoppingToken);
```

That keeps the dispatch provable: the compiler sees `PlaceOrder`, so `ERGO005` still applies if
nothing handles it.

A consumer that dispatches whatever arrives loses that:

```csharp
IMessage message = Deserialize(delivery);
await messageMediator.DispatchAsync(message, stoppingToken);   // opaque
```

This works, and sometimes it is genuinely what you want — a replay tool, a dead-letter drain.
But the static type proves nothing about which message arrives, so reachability can only reason
in aggregate. Turn on `ERGO009` if you want those sites flagged:

```ini
dotnet_diagnostic.ERGO009.severity = warning
```

## Routing by message type

The usual shape is a routing key or a header naming the type. Keep the mapping explicit rather
than resolving a `Type` by name — a computed type cannot be dispatched provably, and if it
reaches a `Register` call it is `ERGO018`:

```csharp
private static readonly Dictionary<string, Func<ReadOnlyMemory<byte>, ICommand>> Routes = new()
{
    ["order.place"]  = body => JsonSerializer.Deserialize<PlaceOrder>(body.Span)!,
    ["order.cancel"] = body => JsonSerializer.Deserialize<CancelOrder>(body.Span)!,
};
```

```csharp
if (!Routes.TryGetValue(delivery.RoutingKey, out var deserialize))
{
    await _channel.BasicNackAsync(delivery.DeliveryTag, false, requeue: false, stoppingToken);
    continue;
}

await mediator.SendAsync(deserialize(delivery.Body), stoppingToken);
```

Each entry names its type in source, so every one of those messages is still visible to the
compiler.

## Acknowledge after the dispatch

Ack when the handler returned, nack when it threw. Acknowledging first turns any failure into
silent data loss.

Whether to requeue is a decision the recipe cannot make for you: requeueing a message that fails
deterministically is an infinite loop. A dead-letter exchange with `requeue: false` is the
common answer.

## Cross-cutting work belongs in interceptors

Resist putting retry, logging and validation in the consumer loop — they apply to every message
and they are what interceptors are for:

```csharp
[Group("queue")]
public sealed class LogQueueDispatches : ICommandFinalInterceptor
{
    public ValueTask HandleAsync(
        IMessage message, object? result, Exception? exception, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        _metrics.Record(message.GetType().Name, succeeded: exception is null);
        return ValueTask.CompletedTask;
    }
}
```

```csharp
await mediator.SendAsync(command, GroupSet.Of("queue"), stoppingToken);
```

The group keeps the queue's pipeline separate from the one an HTTP request gets, without
duplicating a handler.

## Memoized handlers, maybe

A consumer whose handlers are genuinely stateless and whose dependencies are singletons can skip
per-dispatch resolution:

```csharp
services.AddErgosfare(registry => registry
    .ForceMemoizedHandlers()
    .AddCommandModule(module => module.RegisterGenerated()));
```

{% aside type="caution" %}
Only if that is really true. It keeps resolved participants, so a handler depending on anything
scoped captures an instance that outlives the scope that produced it. Most consumers touch a
database, which makes this the wrong choice.
{% /aside %}

## Publishing what happened

If the handler announces its work with an event, publish **after** the state is committed —
otherwise a subscriber can act on something that later rolls back. See the
[Unit of work recipe](/ergosfare.docs/preview/recipes/unit-of-work).

## Shutting down

Pass `stoppingToken` into the dispatch so a shutdown cancels in-flight work rather than being
waited out. If a message must not be interrupted halfway, do not pass it — but then nack on
shutdown so the broker redelivers.
